//? Title: Find stable density clusters with HDBSCAN
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Track persistent clusters and leave isolated points as noise

The synthetic sample contains two groups of six points. The left group
is tighter than the right group, and three points sit far from both
groups. HDBSCAN can select branches that persist over different
density ranges. This example uses a minimum cluster size of three and
counts the point itself when finding its third-neighbor core distance.

Each mutual-reachability edge is weighted by the largest of the
Euclidean distance and the two endpoint core distances. The minimum
spanning tree determines the single-linkage hierarchy for those
weights. Condensation drops child branches smaller than the minimum
cluster size. Excess of mass compares the stability of a retained
branch with the combined stability of its retained descendants.

```{cpp, id=hdbscan-persistent-groups, file="figures/hdbscan_reachability_tree.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/hdbscan.hpp>

#include <array>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    namespace dm = datamunge;
    const dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> points(15, 2, {
        -3.03, .01, -3.10, -.10, -2.92, .04, -3.12, .03, -2.96, -.08, -3.02, .13,
         2.72, .16,  3.23, -.21,  3.18, .31,  2.83, -.28,  2.94, .23,  3.22, .05,
         0., 5.3,   -8., -3.,   10., 2.,
    });
    const auto fit = dm::stats::fit_hdbscan(points, {.min_cluster_size = 3});
    std::println("| Cluster | Members at birth | Birth density | Stability |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::int64_t label = 0;
    for (const auto& c : fit.clusters)
        if (c.selected)
            std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", label++, c.birth_size,
                c.birth_lambda, c.stability);

    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (const auto& edge : fit.minimum_spanning_tree)
        plot.line({points(edge.first, 0), points(edge.second, 0)},
            {points(edge.first, 1), points(edge.second, 1)}, "", {143, 156, 169}, 1.4);
    const std::array<dm::graphics::RGBA, 3> colors{{{29, 94, 150}, {175, 72, 43}, {87, 91, 100}}};
    std::size_t noise = 0;
    for (std::int64_t group = -1; group < label; ++group) {
        std::vector<double> x, y;
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < points.rows(); ++i)
            if (fit.labels[i] == group) {
                x.push_back(points(i, 0));
                y.push_back(points(i, 1));
            }
        if (group == -1)
            noise = x.size();
        plot.points(x, y, group < 0 ? "Noise" : std::format("Cluster {}", group),
            colors[group < 0 ? 2 : static_cast<std::size_t>(group)], group < 0 ? 5 : 4);
    }
    std::println("\n{} points have the noise label `-1`.", noise);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.title("Selected density clusters and noise")
        .x_label("First coordinate").y_label("Second coordinate")
        .x_limits(-9.2, 11.2).y_limits(-3.8, 6.2)
        .size(740, 420).save_svg("build/hdbscan_reachability_tree.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Cluster | Members at birth | Birth density | Stability |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 6 | 0.177 | 45.292 |
| 1 | 6 | 0.177 | 17.213 |

3 points have the noise label `-1`.
\image(figures/hdbscan_reachability_tree.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Points are colored by their fitted labels. Gray lines trace the mutual-reachability minimum spanning tree.)
\alttext(Two dense groups near horizontal coordinates minus three and plus three are selected as separate clusters. Three distant points with gray markers are labeled noise.)

Both selected groups are born at the same split density. The tighter
left group persists to higher density thresholds and has greater
stability. The three isolated points leave the hierarchy before
either selected cluster is born and receive the noise label.

[Campello, Moulavi, Zimek, and Sander (2015)|https://doi.org/10.1145/2733381]
